--- Robin Lee Powell wrote:
> "na na cei" is very far from what you want.

{na} for "one" reminds me of Loglan numbers. If I remember
correctly they are:

0 no
1 ne (oNE)

2 to (TwO) 
3 te (ThreE) (TrEs)

4 fo (FOur)
5 fe (FivE)

6 so
7 se (SEven) (SietE)

8 vo
9 ve         (nueVE)

Easier to learn, but also easier to confuse in actual use.

>
> That's an *excellent* use of ji'e, though.

It is? It seems extremely metaphorical to me. 

li papaci'e cu jimte lo nu mi se salbu lo valsi
11% is a limit/bound/extreme of my knowing words.

?

Maybe {kuspe} gives a better metaphor:

lo nu mi se slabu lo valsi cu kuspe li papace'i
"My knowing of words extends over 11%"
"11% is the extent to which I know words."

But {papace'i lo valsi}, "11% of the words" is
much more straightforward.

mu'o mi'e xorxes



		
